// E0203: a path may not silently drop TRAILING module segments.
//
// `Widget` is declared in `Probe::Lib::Helper`. `Probe::Lib` declares nothing.
// Spec §5.1: "the first segment resolves in scope; the remainder is rooted ...
// no substring matching, no suffix matching. A path that does not resolve is
// an error with a suggestion, not an invitation to search."
//
// This COMPILED until 2026-08-04. `module_ns_matches_prefix` accepted the
// written qualifier anywhere it occurred in the declaring module's path as a
// whole-segment run, so `Probe::Lib` matched `Probe::Lib::Helper` at an
// interior alignment and the path resolved to a type that `Probe::Lib` does
// not declare. The matcher is now anchored to the END of the module
// namespace, which is a suffix test rather than a search.
//
// The sibling tolerance that must NOT regress -- omitting LEADING segments,
// so `future` still names `std::future` -- is pinned by the runtime controls
// in tests/tests/projects/resolution_scope. Losing that shows up there, not
// here.
![config(testing)]
![config(negative, E0203)]
namespace CryoTests::Negative::E0203PathDropsTrailingSegment;
type struct Widget { v: i64; }
function cf_use() -> void {
    const _w: CryoTests::Negative::Widget = CryoTests::Negative::Widget { v: 1 };   //~ ERROR[E0203] cannot find type `CryoTests::Negative::Widget` in
}
function main() -> int { return 0; }
